.TH GREP 1
.SH NAME
grep \- search a file for a pattern
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B grep
[
.B \-cinsv
]
.I pattern
[
.I file ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Grep
searches the input
.I files
(standard input default)
for lines that match the
.IR pattern ,
a regular expression as defined in
.IR regexp (6).
Each line matching the pattern is selected,
and each selected line is copied to the standard output.
.PP
The options are:
.TP
.B \-c
Print only a count of matching lines.
.TP
.B \-i
Ignore alphabetic case distinctions.
Matched lines are printed in their original form.
.TP
.B \-n
Mark each printed line with its line number counted in its file.
.TP
.B \-s
Say nothing about a file that cannot be opened.
Selected lines are still printed.
.TP
.B \-v
Reverse: print lines that do not match the pattern.
.PD
.PP
Output lines are tagged by file name when there is more than one
input file.
Under
.B \-c
the count is tagged the same way.
.PP
Care should be taken when
using the shell metacharacters
.B $*[^|()=\e
in
.IR pattern .
It is safest to enclose the
entire expression
in single quotes.
.SH SOURCE
.B cmd/grep/main.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sed (1),
.IR regexp (6),
.IR regexp (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The exit status is empty if any lines are selected, and
.B "no match"
when none are.
A file that cannot be opened is reported on standard error
and does not change the status.
A pattern that does not compile exits
.BR "bad pattern" .
.SH BUGS
There is no
.BR \-e ,
so a pattern that begins with a minus is read as a set of flags.
A newline in the pattern is not an alternative.
There is no
.B \-l
and no
.BR \-h .
